%S A401300 12,21,27,28,39,49,52,57,64,75,76,91,93,100,111,124,129,133,148,169,
%T A401300 172,175,183,201,217,219,237,244,247,259,268,291,292,301,309,316,325,
%U A401300 327,343,361,363,381,388,403,412,417,427,436,453,469,471,475,481,484
%N A401300 Numbers k such that some Eisenstein integer of norm k has exactly four divisors up to associates.
%C A401300 Here w = exp(2*Pi*i/3) and N(a + b*w) = a^2 - a*b + b^2. A nonzero nonunit Eisenstein integer z has product of divisors equal to z^2 (up to a unit) iff z has exactly four divisors up to associates, so these are the norms of nonunit multiplicatively perfect Eisenstein integers. Analog of A007422 and A397070.
%C A401300 z has four divisors iff z is a product of two non-associate Eisenstein primes or the cube of an Eisenstein prime.
%C A401300 In Z[w], 3 ramifies, primes p == 1 (mod 3) split, and primes q == 2 (mod 3) stay prime with norm q^2. So membership depends only on the factorization of k.
%C A401300 9 is not a term: every Eisenstein integer of norm 9 is an associate of 3 = -w^2*(1 - w)^2, which has three divisors.
%C A401300 The first term in common with A397070 is 169.
%D A401300 K. Ireland and M. Rosen, A Classical Introduction to Modern Number Theory, 2nd ed., Springer, 1990, Chapter 9.
%H A401300 Karan Sharma, <a href="/A401300/b401300.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401300 J. Sándor, <a href="https://emis.de/ft/20122">On multiplicatively perfect numbers</a>, J. Inequal. Pure Appl. Math. 2(1) (2001), Article 3.
%H A401300 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EisensteinInteger.html">Eisenstein Integer</a>.
%F A401300 k is a term iff k is 27, p^2, p^3, q^6, 3*p, 3*q^2, p*p', p*q^2 or q^2*q'^2, where p, p' are distinct primes == 1 (mod 3) and q, q' are distinct primes == 2 (mod 3).
%e A401300 12 is a term: 2 - 2*w = 2*(1 - w) has norm 12 and divisors 1, 1 - w, 2, 2 - 2*w.
%e A401300 27 is a term: (1 - w)^3 has norm 27 and divisors 1, 1 - w, (1 - w)^2, (1 - w)^3.
%e A401300 49 is a term: 7 = (3 + w)*(2 - w) has norm 49 and divisors 1, 3 + w, 2 - w, 7.
%e A401300 64 is a term: 2 is an Eisenstein prime, and 8 has norm 64 and divisors 1, 2, 4, 8.
%t A401300 t[{p_, e_}] := (p == 3 && e == 1) || (Mod[p, 3] == 1 && e == 1) || (Mod[p, 3] == 2 && e == 2);
%t A401300 isok[n_] := Module[{f = FactorInteger[n]},
%t A401300   Which[Length[f] == 1, With[{p = f[[1, 1]], e = f[[1, 2]]},
%t A401300      (p == 3 && e == 3) || (Mod[p, 3] == 1 && 2 <= e <= 3) || (Mod[p, 3] == 2 && e == 6)],
%t A401300    Length[f] == 2, t[f[[1]]] && t[f[[2]]],
%t A401300    True, False]];
%t A401300 Select[Range[2, 500], isok]
%o A401300 (Python)
%o A401300 from sympy import factorint
%o A401300 def isok(n):
%o A401300     f = sorted(factorint(n).items())
%o A401300     t = lambda p, e: (p == 3 and e == 1) or (p % 3 == 1 and e == 1) or (p % 3 == 2 and e == 2)
%o A401300     if len(f) == 1:
%o A401300         p, e = f[0]
%o A401300         return (p == 3 and e == 3) or (p % 3 == 1 and e in (2, 3)) or (p % 3 == 2 and e == 6)
%o A401300     return len(f) == 2 and t(*f[0]) and t(*f[1])
%Y A401300 Cf. A003136, A007422, A007955, A397070.
%K A401300 nonn,new
%O A401300 1,1
